Search a 2D Matrix II
Problem Description
This solution is for efficiently searching for a target value in an m x n matrix, which has the following properties:

Each row is sorted in ascending order from left to right.
Each column is sorted in ascending order from top to bottom.
The task is to return True if the target value exists in the matrix and False otherwise.

Example
Consider the matrix:

csharp
Copy code
matrix = [
    [1, 4, 7, 11, 15],
    [2, 5, 8, 12, 19],
    [3, 6, 9, 16, 22],
    [10, 13, 14, 17, 24],
    [18, 21, 23, 26, 30]
]
If the target is 5, the algorithm will return True.

If the target is 20, the algorithm will return False.

Approach
The algorithm starts from the bottom-left corner of the matrix, as this position allows for optimal searching based on the properties of the matrix.

Key Observations:
If the current value is greater than the target, move up the row.
If the current value is less than the target, move right to the next column.
This reduces the possible search space and leads to an efficient solution.

Pseudocode:
Start from the last row and the first column (matrix[n-1][0]).
Compare the current element:
If it equals the target, return True.
If it is less than the target, move right to the next column.
If it is greater than the target, move up to the previous row.
Repeat until the row index becomes negative or the column index exceeds the matrix's bounds.

Example Walkthrough
For the matrix:

csharp
Copy code
[
    [1, 4, 7, 11, 15],
    [2, 5, 8, 12, 19],
    [3, 6, 9, 16, 22],
    [10, 13, 14, 17, 24],
    [18, 21, 23, 26, 30]
]
Target = 5:
Start at matrix[4][0] = 18. Since 18 > 5, move up to matrix[3][0].
Now matrix[3][0] = 10. Since 10 > 5, move up to matrix[2][0].
Now matrix[2][0] = 3. Since 3 < 5, move right to matrix[2][1].
Now matrix[2][1] = 6. Since 6 > 5, move up to matrix[1][1].
Now matrix[1][1] = 5. Since 5 == 5, return True.
Target = 20:
Start at matrix[4][0] = 18. Since 18 < 20, move right to matrix[4][1].
Now matrix[4][1] = 21. Since 21 > 20, move up to matrix[3][1].
Now matrix[3][1] = 13. Since 13 < 20, move right to matrix[3][2].
Continue searching until the algorithm terminates with False, as 20 is not present.